A||N||18||normalicy||Engine out the top?||||1||
Z||000000||normalicy||03-08-2001||06:26 AM||||I've heard of people taking their 2.5L engine out the top, but am wondering if it's worth the trouble?  In any case, I am wondering what made it possible?<P>------------------<BR>What is normal anyway?||||reg||1
Z||000001||KRMFiero||03-08-2001||06:55 AM||||If i were you just take it out the bottom, I am 16 years old and i did it by myself (1 day, 1 night), no help from anyone.  It was VERY Easy...  then it took me about another 30 minutes to seperate engine from tranny/craddle<P>Taking the engine out the top from what i would thing you would still have to disconnect the same stuff then undo all the bell housing bolts (would be hard to do while inside the car) then put sometype of engine lift on the engine and hook it up so the chain had soem tension on it, then unbolt the engine mounts and you would have to have a few people, have some people with crow bars or something seporating the engine from tranny, have another person slowly lifting the lift and have another person pushign the lift, engine to the passanger side more then i would think it would lift right out<P><BR>I'm not sure though, but you will be surprized how easy it is taking it out the bottom, i didnt have any/much trouble at all, hardest thing was one of my craddle bolts was turnign with the rubber bushing,  i just sprayed it with some Liqiud Wrench, took a hammer and the actual rench, turned and lightly taped on it<P><BR>hope this helps<BR>Kyle<P>------------------<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2M4 SE (Red)<BR>1985 Pontiac Fiero 2M4 SE (White)<P>     <IMG SRC="http://members.aol.com/~Physcokyle/sig2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Black88GT||03-08-2001||01:54 PM||||Since I had never done an engine swap before, I got a mechanic to help me with my 3.4.  He had done it before out of the top and he got it out in under 1 hour.  It looked very easy, but you have to have a crane or something to get it out and put it in.<P>your call||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroSEt||03-08-2001||02:21 PM||||BlackGT88... IS Correct!!  I had never pulled any type of motor at all... I pulled it bymeself in 2hr's and 15 minutes out of the top and that was starting with the removal of the decklid. Please at least think about it.  <BR>Maybe some of the MORE exp. Fiero Junkies could help you out here.<BR>Good Luck how ever you do it!!! <BR>                  Steve aka 1FieroGTRacingInc||||reg||1
Z||000004||normalicy||03-09-2001||02:21 AM||||Maybe I should have mentioned that I do have a ceiling mounted hoist and a large quantity of tools to destroy.  I plan on removing the hood no matter what.  <P>I'm doing an engine swap and will not be needing the transaxle.  Didn't want to take out more than necessary.  Just making sure it's physically possible and also possible to do in a shorter amount of time than pulling it from underneath.<P>Since it appears to be possible, are there any helpful hints to keep me from doing anything stupid?  Thanks all.<P>------------------<BR>What is normal anyway?||||reg||1
Z||000005||ThatRickGuy||03-09-2001||02:45 PM||||take off the alt and AC brackets, and avoid the rear window :P I did mine out the bottom, me and a friend w/ a hoist, took probrably about 8 hours to have the engine out and completly striped w/ the craddle back in the car so we could wheel it out of the shop. Not too bad considering we're a pair of shade tree (at best) mechanics. On a side note, I can strip and rebuild a Dell GX in under 30 minutes.||||reg||1
Z||000006||normalicy||03-10-2001||02:57 AM||||Man, you're fast.  Takes me at least 45 minutes.  Really, thanks for the info anyhow.<P>I have one week and counting before the removal, give me your best info on top removal please.  I know that there are some people that don't go by the book.<P>------------------<BR>What is normal anyway?||||reg||1
Z||000007||normalicy||03-11-2001||02:55 AM||||bump, anyone know what I should do about the replacement engine that has been sitting 2 years besides lubricating the cylinders through the spark plug holes a little and turning it with fresh oil by hand?||||reg||1
Z||000008||KRMFiero||03-11-2001||08:59 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by normalicy:<BR><B>bump, anyone know what I should do about the replacement engine that has been sitting 2 years besides lubricating the cylinders through the spark plug holes a little and turning it with fresh oil by hand?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well of course the mistery oil in the cylinders, cheak the belts, replace plugs,wires,rotor  degreese it, basic things you do when you tune up any car.<P>Kyle||||reg||1
Z||000009||normalicy||03-11-2001||10:32 AM||||Gotcha, will do.||||reg||1
Z||000010||862M4inCA||03-11-2001||12:35 PM||||Well, there are a couple things to consider when doing engine removal from either the top or bottom. Mostly a "give and take" type situation. When pulling from the bottom (the way I removed mine) you have to disconnect all the rear suspension equipment (brakes, struts, cables, etc) which wasn't difficult since fortunately none of the bolts on mine were seized up. or, if you plan to remove from the top (which I learned when reinstalling mine) you need to remove everything bolted to the sides of the motor( alternator and top bracket, waterpump, intake and exhaust manifolds, etc) to be able to clear the decklid hinges and dogbone mounting block on the trunk wall. Just some things to consider for removal since one method will require removal of some things, and the other requires removal of others (in my experience)||||reg||1
Z||000011||normalicy||03-11-2001||02:59 PM||||Oh, if that's all, then I'll go for top removal any time (unless I'm doing the transmission too).  I expected to take off the alternator, compressor, etc..<P>------------------<BR>What is normal anyway?||||reg||1
Z||000012||Fierowrecker||03-11-2001||05:54 PM||||Hey Normalicy!<BR>  I have always gone through the top on the 4 cyl...<BR>Haven't done a 6 yet...<BR>  Remove as much accesories before removal, but pull the transaxle with the engine...<BR>If you try to seperate the trans in the vehicle, it is the pits rejoining in the vehicle...<BR>  Check out my webpage for pics... <A HREF="http://www.walnutgrovefarm.net" TARGET=_blank>www.walnutgrovefarm.net</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||Jay||03-11-2001||06:06 PM||||Why wouldn't you want to go from the bottom?  If you're gonna swap engines why not paint the cradle and suspension while you're at it?  I've done it from the bottom by myself as well putting a Northstar back in it's place.  If you're by yourself or if it is gonna go in and out several times before staying in, then consider going from the bottom.  You can use the ceiling hoist to lift the car once you've placed a trolley under the engine and disconnected everything.  I just used two jacks under each side of the car until the engine cleared through the wheel well.<BR>Basically, it's what you feel comfortable with.  Good Luck!||||reg||1
Z||000014||Tryxalon||03-11-2001||08:08 PM||||From Archie's V8 site  <BR> <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com/arch8.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.v8archie.com/arch8.htm</A> <P>"7. I highly recommend on '84 thru '87 Fieros that you take the rear suspension out with the engine assembly, it makes reassembly must easier. To do this you will need to remove the calipers from the spindles and hang them, and you will need to remove the 3 bolts at the tops of the strut assemblies (shock towers). You can go either way on the '88 Fiero. Either way don't remove the spindle to strut attachment bolts unless you plan to do a wheel alignment after you have reassembled your car. <P>8. I also recommend on '85 thru '88 Fieros that you take the wiring harness out with the engine (this is way too difficult on '84's). This saves you from having to re-route a bunch of wires when reassembling the car. Some of these connections: I.E. starter, alternator, A/C etc. are real hard to connect and dis-connect in the car. You can remove the center console and disconnect the harness from the computer by just un-plugging a few easy to get to wires." <P>You need to read the entire instructions, but you get the idea. <P>I followed his instructions using a rented Engine hoist, removed the engine in less than 2 hours last fall when I replaced the clutch in my 85. <P>Quick, clean, simple. I was the only one working on it, I had a print of his page and the Haynes Manual. I started Saturday 8 AM as I left to get the hoist, I test drove the car at 2PM. I had never worked on a Fiero before. (but I have been working on cars for several years) <P>For my money it is so much easier than taking it out the top, or "cocking the cradle" or anything else.  Plus everything is SO easy to get to when all of it is out. <BR>  <P>||||reg||6
Z||000015||normalicy||03-12-2001||02:26 AM||||Jeez, now I'm starting to reconsider.  Yeah, the only real problem that I could see was hooking up the transmission.||||reg||1
Z||000016||normalicy||03-20-2001||02:49 AM||||OK, after much deliberation, I decided to take the engine out the bottom.  It was actually much easier than it sounds when described.  Unbolting the shocks from top really helped too (not to mention having a ceiling mounted hoist).  The old engine did some major rod throwing.  There was a 6"x6" hole in the block plus the standard 5 or 6 in the oil pan.<P>Now for a new question.  The engine I'm replacing it with is from an '84 Fiero.  The car is an '85.  Will there be computer problems with this setup.  The old engine had a different distributor, so I swapped in the one from the bad engine.  Also, from what I hear, the '84 engines had a different crank shaft, didn't use roller lifters, and had different bearings.  Those are the only differences that I saw.  Thanks for all the help guys.<P>------------------<BR>What is normal anyway?||||reg||1
Z||000017||skitime||03-20-2001||09:17 AM||||Sounds like you need to start a new thread on the engine stuff.  You will get more responses than asking this under a thread on removing engines. ||||reg||1
